k263 gave the rare commands (move, handoff, needs, materialize, import, repair)
error messages that end in the invocation that would have worked, built from the
words the caller typed. It deliberately stopped there. Two messages with the same
disease were left standing because they belong to the frequent commands:

## What is left

1. `Task $id not found` raised from `update_task_guarded`
   (App::karr::Role::TaskMutation). Shared by move, edit, delete, archive and
   handoff, so it could not be fixed inside k263 without pulling the frequent
   commands in. `karr needs` got its own not-found line as part of k263 --
   "Task 999 not found on this board:" followed by `karr list --compact` -- and
   that is the shape this one should take too.

2. `Unknown command: $unknown` + "Run 'karr --help' to see the available
   commands." in App::karr. The way out is quoted inside prose rather than
   spelled as a line to copy. It is already last, so this is content, not
   placement.

3. Whatever the same sweep turns up in list, show, create and edit -- read them
   cold, the way an agent meets them.

## Rules, unchanged from k263

Real id, real status, the caller's own words. Only the value the caller still has
to supply stays a placeholder, and only when at least one token in the line is
real -- a suggestion that carries nothing the caller typed is a generic example
and is not printed at all.

The suggestion is the last line. App::karr::Error/command_hint renders it;
App::karr::Error/clean_error keeps it through the batch runner's first-line
reduction. Exit codes (ADR 0002) and --json payloads stay untouched.

